In the fourth iteration of the program, we connected the water sensor to the breadboard, making sure it works as intended.
In iterations five and six, we continued adjusting the resistance measurements to ensure the stepper only moved when water was detected.
We also made sure the LED turned on when water was sensed.
We built scaffolding to support the stepper and lid as it moved.
We ended the day with testing our prototype, including programming a resistance test and a breath test. 
We also wrote a program that recalibrates our stepper when the angle of movement was inaccurate.

